474
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:07,820
This machine cycles a sandpaper-like material

475
00:34:07,821 --> 00:34:10,780
called Trizac back and forth across the socks.

476
00:34:12,060 --> 00:34:14,380
Most are tested over the course of two days,

477
00:34:14,660 --> 00:34:17,660
but some very hardy socks will make it more than a month.

478
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:22,160
The team also conducts research on innovations

479
00:34:22,161 --> 00:34:24,660
that claim to do everything from preventing blisters

480
00:34:24,661 --> 00:34:26,940
to neutralizing stinky feet.

481
00:34:26,941 --> 00:34:32,840
Foot odor is caused by bacteria that grows in moist areas.

482
00:34:32,841 --> 00:34:36,080
And your feet is a perfect place because it's inside the shoe.

483
00:34:36,600 --> 00:34:37,660
You've got sweat.

484
00:34:37,780 --> 00:34:38,500
You've got moisture.

485
00:34:39,020 --> 00:34:40,420
It's a perfect breeding ground.

486
00:34:41,580 --> 00:34:45,040
There are 250,000 sweat glands in one human foot.

487
00:34:46,520 --> 00:34:51,820
Bacteria on the skin consume the sweat and excrete waste that causes the strong, foul odor.

488
00:34:51,821 --> 00:34:58,120
Scientists have found that knitting silver nanoparticles into socks helps solve the problem.

489
00:34:59,740 --> 00:35:04,900
The silver has inherent antimicrobial properties that enable it to kill bacteria.

490
00:35:06,740 --> 00:35:10,860
And it can comprise from 2 to 80 percent of these odor-eating socks.

491
00:35:12,880 --> 00:35:17,200
While the thread's technology is new, the cure is a time-honored tradition.

492
00:35:17,201 --> 00:35:23,800
I've been told that one of the reasons a lot of the early sailors used to use silver

493
00:35:23,801 --> 00:35:29,200
canteens is because they knew of its ability to kill a lot of the microbes.

494
00:35:29,201 --> 00:35:34,700
So if you're using 100-year-old technology now just to make better socks, that's essentially

495
00:35:34,701 --> 00:35:36,960
what we're doing.

496
00:35:36,961 --> 00:35:41,700
Making socks that are good for our feet can be a strain on the arms and wrists.

497
00:35:41,701 --> 00:35:47,000
So workers at the Kaiser Rock plant do exercises at shift changes to prevent carpal tunnel syndrome.

498
00:35:47,001 --> 00:35:53,560
But then it's back to work, steaming, folding, and packaging the socks.

499
00:35:54,520 --> 00:35:59,240
So now we've been through the entire production process, and we end up with a finished product.

500
00:35:59,720 --> 00:36:02,246
And I have a couple examples to show you.

501
00:36:02,247 --> 00:36:05,880
First, under the no-nonsense name, beautiful women's socks.

502
00:36:06,680 --> 00:36:11,540
And under the men's name in Burlington, great socks, great quality, all made in the United States.

503
00:36:11,541 --> 00:36:18,000
Another technological breakthrough on the horizon for sock making promises to keep us

504
00:36:18,001 --> 00:36:20,000
light on our feet.

505
00:36:20,001 --> 00:36:26,980
A fabric called holofiber harnesses the power of the sun to help our socks relieve pain

506
00:36:26,981 --> 00:36:30,580
and increase energy and strength.

507
00:36:30,581 --> 00:36:36,020
Introduced into the world of sock making in 2003, it contains microscopic lenses embedded

508
00:36:36,021 --> 00:36:39,980
into the actual polyester fibers.

509
00:36:39,981 --> 00:36:44,980
These lenses channel available sunlight permeating the exposed part of the sock to increase blood

510
00:36:44,981 --> 00:36:51,520
flow and oxygen saturation in the foot.

511
00:36:51,521 --> 00:36:57,800
The holofiber uses light not only from the sun, but perhaps also from the foot itself.

512
00:36:57,801 --> 00:37:02,920
The theory is this, that your body gives off energy all the time in the form of both heat

513
00:37:02,921 --> 00:37:03,920
and light.

514
00:37:03,921 --> 00:37:08,700
People who wear socks that have holofiber talk about having more energy, they're more

515
00:37:08,701 --> 00:37:12,500
They wear them on long flights, and their feet don't swell.

516
00:37:12,600 --> 00:37:16,300
In this test, we see one foot standing on hollow fiber

517
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:19,640
and one foot on standard cotton fiber.

518
00:37:19,740 --> 00:37:22,940
Both feet have been exposed to cold temperatures.

519
00:37:23,040 --> 00:37:25,460
The foot on the hollow fiber reaches normal temperature

520
00:37:25,560 --> 00:37:29,560
almost immediately, while the other one takes several minutes.

521
00:37:31,760 --> 00:37:34,360
If only hollow fiber could help you locate your sock

522
00:37:34,460 --> 00:37:37,460
after it mysteriously fails to emerge from your dryer.

523
00:37:37,461 --> 00:37:40,100
your dryer.

524
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:43,160
While you're searching for it, ponder this.

525
00:37:43,260 --> 00:37:44,700
What's the secret ingredient added

526
00:37:44,800 --> 00:37:47,100
to the underwear of firefighters, race car

527
00:37:47,200 --> 00:37:52,000
drivers, and football players?

528
00:37:52,100 --> 00:37:55,620
If you unraveled all the yarn in an average pair of men's socks,

529
00:37:55,720 --> 00:37:59,420
it would stretch longer than one mile.

530
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:02,120
Underwear will return on Modern Marvels.

531
00:38:07,460 --> 00:38:11,460
As these race car drivers thunder around the course at perilous speeds,

532
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:15,300
their special underwear just may be saving their lives.

533
00:38:17,080 --> 00:38:17,980
Wondering how?

534
00:38:19,080 --> 00:38:26,340
Over the course of a race, the temperature inside the driver's compartment reaches as high as a scorching 140 degrees Fahrenheit.

535
00:38:27,580 --> 00:38:31,080
Heat stroke can occur if the body's temperature rises above 105 degrees.

536
00:38:31,800 --> 00:38:34,720
But the danger kicks in before that fatal threshold.

537
00:38:34,721 --> 00:38:41,580
When you get too hot, your blood starts to go to your skin for cooling, for perspiration.

538
00:38:41,581 --> 00:38:45,240
And when that happens, the heart is pumping to get it there.

539
00:38:45,241 --> 00:38:48,900
And then your major organs, including your brain, are not functioning on the blood supply

540
00:38:48,901 --> 00:38:49,900
that no one needs.

541
00:38:49,901 --> 00:38:56,000
This can impair decision-making ability, which can prove deadly in a high-speed race.

542
00:38:56,001 --> 00:38:58,040
The solution is the cool shirt.

543
00:38:58,041 --> 00:39:01,520
Cold water is pumped in through all the tubes.

544
00:39:01,521 --> 00:39:03,020
In my back.

545
00:39:03,021 --> 00:39:05,040
and the front side of me.

546
00:39:06,240 --> 00:39:08,200
I basically wear it underneath my fire suit.

547
00:39:09,480 --> 00:39:16,400
It's a simple cotton t-shirt that has 45 to 60 feet of 1 1⁄8 inch vinyl tubing sewn onto the front and back.

548
00:39:18,740 --> 00:39:22,540
We break the cooling of the garment into four cooling zones,

549
00:39:22,840 --> 00:39:27,360
and each one of the lines is within two inches of another line,

550
00:39:27,700 --> 00:39:29,720
so you don't have hot spots or cold spots.

551
00:39:30,680 --> 00:39:35,140
Two small hoses on the shirt plug into a connector on the driver's side of the car.

552
00:39:35,520 --> 00:39:41,900
As I hop in, you'll see the tubes connected to right here.

553
00:39:42,060 --> 00:39:45,140
And basically all I have to do is plug these in.

554
00:39:45,880 --> 00:39:53,060
This connects the shirt to a cooler that circulates four ounces of 45-degree water through the entire shirt every 10 seconds.

555
00:39:54,320 --> 00:39:55,980
This is a really basic system.

556
00:39:55,981 --> 00:39:58,200
This is where the ice and the water live.

557
00:39:58,201 --> 00:40:01,080
There's a pump that pumps cold water through these lines

558
00:40:01,081 --> 00:40:03,540
over to my cool shirt just plugged into the car

559
00:40:03,541 --> 00:40:04,500
and into my suit.

560
00:40:04,501 --> 00:40:07,000
Pretty basic.

561
00:40:07,001 --> 00:40:09,340
Rich Schaefer, a medical supply salesman

562
00:40:09,341 --> 00:40:12,040
and racing enthusiast, began manufacturing

563
00:40:12,041 --> 00:40:13,720
the cool shirt in 1998.

564
00:40:16,880 --> 00:40:18,680
He was inspired by a pioneering effort

565
00:40:18,681 --> 00:40:21,300
during the early days of the space program.

566
00:40:21,301 --> 00:40:25,260
It started back in the 60s with NASA, with the astronauts,

567
00:40:25,261 --> 00:40:28,260
and cooling them off when they put their suits on.

568
00:40:28,261 --> 00:40:31,500
They could not get rid of the body heat.

569
00:40:31,501 --> 00:40:34,200
Ness's researchers found that when the outside temperature

570
00:40:34,201 --> 00:40:38,700
rises up to 95 degrees, a person can make up to 60 mistakes

571
00:40:38,701 --> 00:40:40,900
an hour.

572
00:40:40,901 --> 00:40:43,500
Bringing the temperature down just 5 degrees

573
00:40:43,501 --> 00:40:48,320
reduces the number of mistakes to an average of 9 per hour.

574
00:40:48,321 --> 00:40:50,560
Ness's liquid-cooled garment consisted

575
00:40:50,561 --> 00:40:55,060
of 300 feet of 3 16-inch tubing sewn onto a mesh body

576
00:40:55,061 --> 00:40:56,600
suit worn under the space suit.

577
00:41:00,440 --> 00:41:02,980
The astronauts carried backpacks that circulated

578
00:41:02,981 --> 00:41:04,440
cooled water through the tubing.

579
00:41:07,840 --> 00:41:12,640
It was first used in 1969 on the Apollo 9 flight.

580
00:41:12,641 --> 00:41:15,420
Rich Schaefer found that in most cases on Earth,

581
00:41:15,421 --> 00:41:18,200
a full body suit wasn't necessary.

582
00:41:18,201 --> 00:41:19,880
Just bringing down the core temperature

583
00:41:19,881 --> 00:41:22,220
at the chest, abdomen, and back areas

584
00:41:22,221 --> 00:41:25,040
alleviates the problems of heat stress and heat stroke.

585
00:41:25,060 --> 00:41:33,340
In Atlanta, Georgia, the cool shirt has been called into active duty for the hazardous materials

586
00:41:33,341 --> 00:41:36,109
team of the Henry County Fire Department.

587
00:41:36,110 --> 00:41:39,620
Their protective suits may keep them safe from toxic

588
00:41:39,621 --> 00:41:44,300
chemicals and other dangers, but they can also send core temperatures skyrocketing.

589
00:41:46,620 --> 00:41:51,560
After returning from a mission, each man hooks up his cool shirt to accelerate his recovery

590
00:41:51,561 --> 00:41:54,460
procedure called rehab.

591
00:41:54,560 --> 00:41:58,960
In the level A suits, you do get extremely hot inside there.

592
00:41:59,060 --> 00:42:00,460
You lose a lot of your body fluids.

593
00:42:00,560 --> 00:42:02,200
And if we keep the core temperature of the personnel

594
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:04,300
down, the quicker we can rehab them.

595
00:42:04,400 --> 00:42:05,900
Really, I can't feel it, you know,

596
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:06,800
circulating anymore.

597
00:42:06,900 --> 00:42:08,720
But I can tell it's there because my temp's going down.

598
00:42:14,820 --> 00:42:16,620
Many firefighters wear a different version

599
00:42:16,720 --> 00:42:19,420
of the cool shirt called the cool vest,

600
00:42:19,421 --> 00:42:22,260
also to rehabilitate after battling a fire.

601
00:42:22,360 --> 00:42:25,660
Recently, a local fire academy here,

602
00:42:25,760 --> 00:42:28,100
they had a death because during training,

603
00:42:28,200 --> 00:42:31,700
heat exhaustion was a problem.

604
00:42:31,800 --> 00:42:34,140
Firefighters go into a burn building,

605
00:42:34,240 --> 00:42:37,340
and in that burn building, they experience temperatures

606
00:42:37,440 --> 00:42:39,300
exceeding 400 degrees.

607
00:42:39,400 --> 00:42:40,700
That's even in training.

608
00:42:40,800 --> 00:42:42,940
They're wearing 70 pounds of gear.

609
00:42:43,040 --> 00:42:47,080
And so they get put under a lot of heat stress.

610
00:42:47,180 --> 00:42:48,620
For these firefighters, the vest

611
00:42:48,621 --> 00:42:51,120
is a lifesaver.

612
00:42:51,121 --> 00:42:54,220
And for many surgeons, it's the ideal way to stay cool

613
00:42:54,221 --> 00:42:59,060
as they save lives during demanding sessions in the OR.

614
00:42:59,061 --> 00:43:02,640
It's like the feeling you get when you dive into a lake

615
00:43:02,641 --> 00:43:03,900
to get refreshed.

616
00:43:03,901 --> 00:43:07,380
If you're working and you know that you have a two hour case

617
00:43:07,381 --> 00:43:09,800
ahead of you, it makes you feel secure

618
00:43:09,801 --> 00:43:12,620
that it will keep you comfortable throughout the procedure,

619
00:43:12,621 --> 00:43:15,580
not only for our comfort, but for the patient's benefit

620
00:43:15,581 --> 00:43:16,480
as well.

621
00:43:16,481 --> 00:43:18,320
I would say don't go into the operating

622
00:43:18,321 --> 00:43:19,949
room without it.

623
00:43:19,950 --> 00:43:27,280
In years to come, cool underwear may also become a must on the gridiron.

624
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:32,480
These football players at Union Grove High School in McDonough, Georgia,

625
00:43:33,040 --> 00:43:35,629
are among those leading the way.

626
00:43:35,630 --> 00:43:40,160
We're out here practicing in 100 degree weather, 95 with

627
00:43:40,161 --> 00:43:42,050
humidity so high here in Georgia.

628
00:43:42,051 --> 00:43:45,200
It's just going to be a great safeguard for these kids.

629
00:43:45,201 --> 00:43:47,920
It's actually really cold, but it's very beneficial.

630
00:43:48,500 --> 00:43:53,160
After a long day's practice like today, it's definitely going to help a lot.

631
00:43:53,860 --> 00:43:54,560
Cooling me off.

632
00:43:54,960 --> 00:43:57,400
It feels really good. Keeps your body hydrated.

633
00:43:58,340 --> 00:44:00,980
Keep the core cool. I like it.

634
00:44:01,940 --> 00:44:04,480
Count this simple yet effective technology

635
00:44:04,481 --> 00:44:09,340
among the many ways that underwear continues to evolve and redefine its limits.

636
00:44:10,140 --> 00:44:14,900
With any luck, the innovators dreaming up ideas for the apparel we can't do without

637
00:44:14,901 --> 00:44:17,800
We'll continue to think outside the boxes.

